## Problem
Async operations in useEffect can cause state updates after component unmount, leading to memory leaks and React warnings.

## Solution
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  async function fetchData() {
    const result = await api.getData();
    if (!cancelled) setState(result);
  }
  fetchData();
  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, []);
```

## Key Insight
This pattern was discovered across 3 different projects where the same memory leak issue appeared. The AbortController approach is even better for fetch requests:
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal }).then(...);
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);
```

## Problem
Passing non-serializable objects (Date, Map, Set, class instances) through Electron IPC silently converts them to plain objects or drops them entirely.

## Solution
Always serialize to JSON-safe types before sending over IPC:
```typescript
// Bad
ipcMain.handle('get-data', () => ({ date: new Date(), items: new Map() }));

// Good
ipcMain.handle('get-data', () => ({
  date: new Date().toISOString(),
  items: Object.fromEntries(myMap)
}));
```

## Impact
This bug caused silent data loss in 2 production features before we identified the root cause.

## Problem
Ignoring backpressure when piping large files through Node.js streams causes memory exhaustion.

## Solution
```typescript
const readable = fs.createReadStream(inputPath);
const writable = fs.createWriteStream(outputPath);

readable.pipe(writable);

writable.on('drain', () => readable.resume());
readable.on('data', (chunk) => {
  if (!writable.write(chunk)) readable.pause();
});
```

## Key Insight
Always use pipeline() from stream/promises for automatic backpressure handling:
```typescript
import { pipeline } from 'stream/promises';
await pipeline(readable, transform, writable);
```

## Problem
`auto-fit` and `auto-fill` behave identically when there are enough items to fill the row, but diverge when items are fewer than columns.

## Key Difference
- `auto-fill`: Creates empty tracks even when no items fill them
- `auto-fit`: Collapses empty tracks to zero width

```css
/* Items stretch to fill available space */
grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));

/* Empty columns remain as gaps */
grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(250px, 1fr));
```

## When to Use
Use `auto-fit` for most responsive layouts. Use `auto-fill` when you need consistent column widths regardless of item count.

## Problem
Unhandled errors in Electron main process IPC handlers crash the entire application.

## Solution
Wrap all IPC handlers in a standardized error boundary:
```typescript
function safeHandle(channel: string, handler: Function) {
  ipcMain.handle(channel, async (event, ...args) => {
    try {
      return { success: true, data: await handler(event, ...args) };
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`[IPC] ${channel} failed:`, error);
      return { success: false, error: error.message };
    }
  });
}
```

## Architecture Decision
This pattern was adopted project-wide after 3 crash incidents. All 47 IPC handlers now use this wrapper.

## Problem
Large monolithic Zustand stores cause unnecessary re-renders across unrelated components.

## Solution
Use selector-based slicing and separate stores per domain:
```typescript
// Instead of one giant store
const useStore = create((set) => ({ agents: [], skills: [], plugins: [], ... }));

// Use domain-specific stores
const useAgentStore = create((set) => ({ items: [], loading: false, fetch: async () => {...} }));
const useSkillStore = create((set) => ({ personal: [], plugin: [], ... }));

// Always use selectors
const agents = useAgentStore((s) => s.items); // Only re-renders when items change
```

## Impact
Reduced unnecessary re-renders by ~60% in the HIMA-AIP dashboard.

## Pattern
Use event-driven architecture with message queues instead of synchronous HTTP calls between microservices.

## Implementation
```typescript
// Publisher
await messageQueue.publish('order.created', {
  orderId: order.id,
  userId: order.userId,
  items: order.items,
  timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
});

// Consumer (separate service)
messageQueue.subscribe('order.created', async (event) => {
  await inventoryService.reserveItems(event.items);
  await notificationService.sendConfirmation(event.userId);
});
```

## Key Insight
This decoupling reduced inter-service latency by 40% and eliminated cascading failures during peak traffic.

## Pattern
Wrap all API responses in a consistent envelope:
```typescript
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: { code: string; message: string; details?: unknown };
  meta?: { page: number; total: number; timestamp: string };
}
```

## Benefits
1. Consistent error handling on the client
2. Pagination metadata always available
3. Easy to add cross-cutting concerns (request ID, timing)

## Implementation
Use middleware to automatically wrap responses:
```typescript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const originalJson = res.json.bind(res);
  res.json = (data) => originalJson({ success: true, data, meta: { timestamp: new Date().toISOString() } });
  next();
});
```

## Technique
Use React DevTools Profiler with "Why did this render?" enabled:

1. Open React DevTools → Profiler tab
2. Click gear → Enable "Record why each component rendered"
3. Start profiling → Interact with the app → Stop
4. Examine flame chart for unnecessary renders

## Common Causes
```typescript
// Bad: New object reference every render
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Good: Memoize or extract
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red' }), []);
<Child style={style} />

// Bad: Inline callback
<Button onClick={() => handleClick(id)} />

// Good: useCallback
const handleClickMemo = useCallback(() => handleClick(id), [id]);
```

## Impact
Profiling revealed that the Dashboard page was re-rendering 47 times on initial load. After fixes, reduced to 3.

## Problem
Prisma's lazy loading causes N+1 queries when accessing relations in loops.

## Solution
Always use `include` or `select` to eagerly load relations:
```typescript
// Bad: N+1
const users = await prisma.user.findMany();
for (const user of users) {
  const posts = await prisma.post.findMany({ where: { authorId: user.id } });
}

// Good: Single query with join
const users = await prisma.user.findMany({
  include: { posts: { select: { id: true, title: true } } }
});
```

## Detection
Enable Prisma query logging:
```typescript
const prisma = new PrismaClient({ log: ['query'] });
```
Look for repeated similar queries in the log output.

## Optimizations Applied
1. **Code splitting**: Separate vendor chunks for React, UI libraries
2. **Tree shaking**: Ensure `sideEffects: false` in package.json
3. **Asset inlining**: Small images (<4KB) inlined as base64
4. **Dependency externalization**: Node.js built-ins excluded from bundle

```typescript
// electron.vite.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  main: {
    plugins: [externalizeDepsPlugin()],
    build: {
      rollupOptions: {
        output: { manualChunks: { vendor: ['electron-store'] } }
      }
    }
  },
  renderer: {
    build: {
      rollupOptions: {
        output: { manualChunks: { react: ['react', 'react-dom'], ui: ['lucide-react'] } }
      }
    }
  }
});
```

## Impact
Bundle size reduced from 12MB to 4.3MB. Cold start time improved by 1.8s.

## Problem
Rendering 1000+ items in a list causes significant jank and high memory usage.

## Solution
Use `@tanstack/react-virtual` for virtualized rendering:
```typescript
import { useVirtualizer } from '@tanstack/react-virtual';

function VirtualList({ items }) {
  const parentRef = useRef(null);
  const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
    count: items.length,
    getScrollElement: () => parentRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => 60,
    overscan: 5,
  });

  return (
    <div ref={parentRef} style={{ height: '400px', overflow: 'auto' }}>
      <div style={{ height: virtualizer.getTotalSize() }}>
        {virtualizer.getVirtualItems().map((vi) => (
          <div key={vi.key} style={{ transform: `translateY(${vi.start}px)` }}>
            {items[vi.index].name}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Impact
Session list rendering went from 2.3s to 16ms for 5000 items.

## Problem
Default Electron apps have no CSP, exposing users to XSS attacks.

## Solution
Set CSP in the main process:
```typescript
session.defaultSession.webRequest.onHeadersReceived((details, callback) => {
  callback({
    responseHeaders: {
      ...details.responseHeaders,
      'Content-Security-Policy': [
        "default-src 'self';",
        "script-src 'self';",
        "style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';",
        "img-src 'self' data: https:;",
        "connect-src 'self' https://api.example.com;"
      ].join(' ')
    }
  });
});
```

## Key Insight
Always test CSP in development mode. Use `report-uri` directive to catch violations before they break features.

## Problem
Storing API keys in renderer process code exposes them in DevTools and source maps.

## Solution
1. Store keys in main process only (never in renderer)
2. Use `electron-store` with encryption:
```typescript
import Store from 'electron-store';
const store = new Store({ encryptionKey: 'your-encryption-key' });
store.set('apiKey', key);
```
3. Proxy API calls through main process:
```typescript
ipcMain.handle('api:call', async (_, endpoint, params) => {
  const key = store.get('apiKey');
  return fetch(endpoint, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` }, ...params });
});
```

## Never Do
- Hardcode keys in source code
- Pass keys to renderer via preload
- Store keys in localStorage or sessionStorage

## Problem
Plugins can inject malicious content through agent descriptions, skill bodies, or command outputs.

## Solution
Sanitize all plugin-provided content before rendering:
```typescript
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

function renderPluginContent(html: string): string {
  return DOMPurify.sanitize(html, {
    ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'br', 'strong', 'em', 'code', 'pre', 'ul', 'ol', 'li', 'a', 'h1', 'h2', 'h3'],
    ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'class'],
    ALLOW_DATA_ATTR: false,
  });
}
```

## Key Insight
Always sanitize on the rendering side, not the storage side. This preserves the original content for legitimate use cases while preventing XSS.

